{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013RBP37MkoWqNbHmMtmBNVJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ete1WTGf8MeFXsbEHXmQH1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\n\\title{Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability\\\\in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale DIF Analysis of MMLU}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that scores from different model generations reflect the same underlying construct. We test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe report four findings.\n(1)~31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts; item-level decomposition shows domain accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points (7.6\\%) of this instability.\n(2)~Web-overlap contamination labels, routinely used as ground truth for item-level detectors, measure global exposure rather than differential contamination; DIF enrichment $\\approx$1.0 against such labels is expected, not failure.\n(3)~We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework ($\\Delta$TPR methodology) with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) and generalizes across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n(4)~DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) yet are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$), serving as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.\nOverall rankings remain highly stable after removing all flagged items ($\\rho\\approx0.997$), confirming that instability does not threaten validity but reveals which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we report that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under equivalent conditions. Psychometrics calls this assumption \\emph{measurement invariance}: an item that is easy for Group~A examinees of a given ability level should be equally easy for Group~B examinees at the same ability level. When this fails---when an item's difficulty shifts after controlling for overall ability---the item exhibits \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF).\n\nDIF analysis is a mature tool in educational and psychological testing, with over six decades of methodological development since Mantel and Haenszel's original proposal~\\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential}. It has been applied to ensure fairness across demographic groups, flag compromised items in high-stakes exams, and validate the temporal stability of test scores. Yet it has seen virtually no application to LLM evaluation, despite the field's growing concern about benchmark contamination and score inflation.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix---5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items---using models released before and after a temporal cutoff as reference and focal groups. This constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis ever conducted on an LLM benchmark. Our key finding is that \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations} (ETS C classification, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$), but this instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho\\approx0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.} This framing is central: DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed across model generations. This is a \\emph{diagnostic signal}, not a quality verdict. The massive ranking stability ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) under 31\\% item removal reveals enormous measurement redundancy in MMLU. The value of DIF analysis lies not in filtering ``bad'' items but in understanding \\emph{what changed} across generations---which capability dimensions are shifting and how.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item An empirical characterization of temporal item instability at unprecedented scale, showing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF that is predominantly item-specific (domain accuracy improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\%).\n    \\item A ground truth meta-critique: web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors, affecting the validity claims of existing methods.\n    \\item A reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation and generalizes across benchmarks.\n    \\item A demonstration that DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding cross-generational capability shifts, not merely a quality filter.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nSeveral methods detect training-set contamination in LLMs. Min-K\\%~\\citep{shi2024detecting} uses token-level perplexity; ConStat~\\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original vs.\\ paraphrased items; n-gram overlap methods check for verbatim memorization. These methods typically validate against web-overlap labels (e.g., Li et al.'s MMLU contamination annotations), which measure whether an item is publicly accessible rather than whether it functions differently across model groups.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated with \\citet{mantel1959statistical} and was formalized for testing contexts by \\citet{holland1988differential}. The ETS classification system~\\citep{zieky1993practical} categorizes DIF magnitude into negligible (A: $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.0$), moderate (B: $1.0\\leq|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.5$), and large (C: $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$). Item-Focused Trees~\\citep{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates. We apply these standard methods without modification; our contribution is empirical, not algorithmic.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Methodology.}\nGrowing Pains~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} uses anchor items for efficient evaluation. METABENCH~\\citep{becker2024metabench} aggregates 5{,}227 models' responses on MMLU, providing the response matrix we analyze. Our DIF analysis complements these efforts by identifying which anchor items are degrading across model generations.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $j$, the MH procedure tests whether the odds of a correct response differ between reference (R) and focal (F) groups after stratifying by total score. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_\\text{MH}$ is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the entries of the $2\\times2$ contingency table at score stratum $k$ and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds ratio is converted to the $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH}).\n\\end{equation}\n\nWe use the ETS classification: items with $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$ and a statistically significant MH $\\chi^2$ test ($p<0.05$) are classified as ETS C (large DIF). The dual requirement of both effect size and statistical significance provides implicit protection against multiple testing~\\citep{zieky1993practical}.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort Definitions.}\nWe define temporal cohorts by model release date: reference (2023) and focal (2024-H1). We also examine ability-based cohorts (Q1 vs.\\ Q5 of total score) and within-family cohorts (e.g., Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3) as sensitivity analyses.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\\label{sec:semisyn}\n\nTo validate DIF detection power, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices. For a set of ``contaminated'' items and a target exploitation rate $p$, we flip $p$-fraction of incorrect focal-group responses to correct, simulating the effect of contamination that differentially benefits newer models.\n\n\\paragraph{$\\Delta$TPR Methodology.}\nDetection power is measured as:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}_\\text{injected}(p) - \\text{TPR}_\\text{baseline}(0),\n\\end{equation}\nisolating the detection power increase attributable to injected contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching.}\nStandard MH uses total score on the same subject as the matching variable. When contamination affects items within that subject, the matching variable is itself contaminated, inflating FPR. We propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: using total score on \\emph{other} MMLU subjects as the matching variable. This eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) while preserving detection power.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Claim 1: Temporal Item Instability}\\label{sec:claim1}\n\n\\subsubsection{Main DIF Analysis (MMLU)}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 items) with temporal cohorts (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1; $n_\\text{ref}=1{,}253$, $n_\\text{foc}=656$).\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary by ETS classification on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nETS Class & $n$ items & \\% & Mean $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nA (negligible) & 8{,}590 & 68.7 & 0.43 \\\\\nB (moderate) & 2{,}641 & 21.1 & 1.24 \\\\\nC (large) & 1{,}277 & 10.2 & 2.31 \\\\\nB+C (flagged) & 3{,}918 & 31.3 & 1.58 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} shows that 31.3\\% of items (3{,}918 / 12{,}508) are flagged as exhibiting moderate or large DIF. A random-split sanity check produces only 0.18\\% flagged items, confirming that the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cohort Sensitivity}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF results under different cohort definitions.}\n\\label{tab:cohort}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $n_\\text{ref}$ & $n_\\text{foc}$ & C\\% & Enrichment \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs 2024-H1) & 1{,}253 & 656 & 31.3 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility (Q1 vs Q5) & $\\sim$1{,}044 & $\\sim$1{,}044 & 2.9 & 1.19 \\\\\nWithin-family (Llama-2 vs 3) & 161 & 308 & 61.3 & --- \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 950 & 950 & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:cohort} reveals three patterns: (1)~temporal cohorts produce substantial DIF (31.3\\%); (2)~ability-based cohorts produce minimal DIF (2.9\\%), indicating that the temporal signal is not an artifact of ability differences; (3)~within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\\ 3) produces the highest DIF (61.3\\%), consistent with rapid capability gains within an architecture family. The near-zero enrichment against Li et al.\\ contamination labels ($0.96\\approx1.0$) motivates the ground truth meta-critique in \\S\\ref{sec:claim2}. Domain-specific DIF correlates modestly with accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of 31.3\\% C\\% (7.6\\%).\n\n\\subsubsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification: DIF and IRT fit.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $n$ models & C\\% & RMSEA & CFI \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.089 & 0.72 \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.088 & 0.72 \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & --- & 0.0 & 0.001 & 0.00 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5{,}226/5{,}227 models). Removing the single non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0, Table~\\ref{tab:arch}). The poor absolute IRT fit (CFI\\,=\\,0.72) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity; 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under 1D IRT. We discuss IRT misspecification in \\S\\ref{sec:limitations}.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 2: Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\\label{sec:claim2}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe enrichment ratio of temporal DIF flags against Li et al.'s web-overlap contamination labels is 0.96---effectively 1.0. This is \\emph{expected}, not evidence of DIF failure: web-overlap labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible (a global property), while DIF measures whether an item functions differently across model cohorts (a differential property). These are fundamentally different constructs.\n\nAny item-level contamination detector validated exclusively against web-overlap labels inherits this mismatch. Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods all use such validation. Our semi-synthetic injection (\\S\\ref{sec:claim3}) confirms that DIF can detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at $p=0.5$), demonstrating that the enrichment$\\approx$1.0 result reflects a ground truth limitation, not a detection limitation.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 3: Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\\label{sec:claim3}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsubsection{Detection Power on MMLU}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nExploitation rate $p$ & Baseline TPR & Injected TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 & 0.326 & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.326 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.326 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.326 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 0.326 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} demonstrates three properties of the framework: (1)~a monotone dose-response relationship from $p=0.3$ to $p=1.0$; (2)~substantial detection power ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at $p=0.5$); and (3)~zero FPR inflation under cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 at all exploitation rates).\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Within-subject vs.\\ cross-subject matching.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=0.5$) & $\\Delta$FPR ($p=1.0$) & FPR stable? \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & 0.457 & +0.186 & No \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.508 & 0.000 & Yes \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:matching} highlights a key methodological finding: within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable, inflating FPR by +0.186. Cross-subject matching eliminates this inflation while actually improving $\\Delta$TPR (+0.508 vs.\\ +0.457), because the uncontaminated matching variable provides a purer ability estimate.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic results (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & C\\% & Sanity C\\% & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard (total score) & 18.9 & 0.3 & +0.818 \\\\\nExternal (MMLU score) & 47.4 & --- & +0.517 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_dif} confirms the framework's portability. GSM8K temporal DIF is lower (18.9\\% vs.\\ 31.3\\% on MMLU), consistent with math benchmarks exhibiting more uniform capability improvement. The semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 on GSM8K exceeds MMLU's +0.674, attributable to GSM8K's single-domain structure enabling cleaner matching.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 4: DIF as Diagnostic Lens}\\label{sec:claim4}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsubsection{Ranking Stability Under DIF Removal}\n\nRemoving all 3{,}918 DIF-flagged items (31.3\\%) from MMLU yields a rank correlation of $\\rho\\approx0.997$ with the original full-benchmark ranking. This confirms massive measurement redundancy: nearly a third of items can be removed with negligible ranking impact.\n\nHowever, DIF removal causes \\emph{more} disruption than random removal of the same number of items ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across 21 conditions, all $p<10^{-4}$), confirming that DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal. They are non-trivial items---but they are not the most ranking-defining items.\n\n\\subsubsection{Circularity Ablation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ranking disruption by removal strategy (representative $z$-scores).}\n\\label{tab:circularity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nRemoval strategy & $z$-score \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & $-9.5$ \\\\\nAccuracy-gap & $-157$ \\\\\nHigh-variance & $-108$ \\\\\nHigh-discrimination & $-16$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:circularity} resolves the potential tautology concern: if DIF items were simply the most informative items, their removal would cause maximal disruption. Instead, DIF removal ($z=-9.5$) is 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than accuracy-gap ($z=-157$) or high-variance ($z=-108$) removal, despite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection. MH ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\n\n\\subsubsection{GSM1K Contamination Gap Correlation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Correlation between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gap ($N=33$ models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value & 95\\% BCa CI & Concern \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ & CI wide \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & $[-0.687, -0.031]$ & Upper bound near zero \\\\\nPartial $r$ & $-0.385$ & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ & Marginal control \\\\\n$N$ & 33 & --- & Underpowered \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm1k} presents suggestive evidence that DIF items resist contamination-based gains: models with larger GSM1K contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$; permutation $p=0.014$). All confidence intervals exclude zero, but the small sample ($N=33$) produces wide CIs. The Spearman CI upper bound ($-0.031$) approaches zero, and we acknowledge this as suggestive evidence requiring replication with $N\\geq100$.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Supplementary Analyses}\\label{sec:supplementary}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe report several supplementary analyses that provide robustness checks and contextual baselines.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Validation}\\label{sec:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability under cohort composition change (RL augmentation).}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort composition & $n$ total & C\\% & Jaccard vs.\\ baseline \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 312 & 30.8 & --- \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL & 789 & 32.1 & 0.72 \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL + reasoning-RL & 802 & 31.5 & 0.91 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAdding 477 alignment-RL models produces stable DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.72 vs.\\ SFT-only baseline; Table~\\ref{tab:rl}). However, only 13 reasoning-RL models were available, making RL robustness validation underpowered. TOST equivalence testing is inconclusive ($p=0.14$, bound $\\pm0.05$). We do not claim RL robustness; alignment-RL stability is reported descriptively.\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Individual detector AUROC by model training type.}\n\\label{tab:rl_auroc}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & SFT & Alignment-RL & Reasoning-RL & DeLong $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.61 & 0.58 & 0.43 & 0.08 \\\\\nConStat & 0.64 & 0.60 & 0.39 & 0.03 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl_auroc} shows that individual contamination detectors (Min-K\\%, ConStat) degrade substantially on reasoning-RL models (AUROC dropping to 0.39--0.43), with ConStat's decline statistically significant (DeLong $p=0.03$). DIF, as a group-level method, cannot be directly compared at the individual-model level.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\\label{sec:constat}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection summary (217 paraphrased MMLU items, 15 models).}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Non-RL models & RL models & All \\\\\n\\midrule\n$n$ models & 11 & 4 & 15 \\\\\nDetection rate (FDR 0.05) & 0.27 & 0.14 & 0.23 \\\\\nMean effect size & 0.74 & 0.31 & 0.61 \\\\\nFPR & 0.05 & 0.08 & 0.06 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat detects 23\\% of items at FDR\\,=\\,0.05 on 15 models with inference access (Table~\\ref{tab:constat}). The near-zero overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.08) confirms that perturbation-based and group-level methods capture different signals. ConStat's requirement for inference access (vs.\\ DIF's use of existing response matrices at scale) limits direct comparison.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\\label{sec:dvd}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD bimodal detection by contamination type (500 items, 12 models, 50 responses/item).}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nContamination type & $n$ items & Bimodal rate & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 150 & 0.31 & 0.08 \\\\\nVariant & 150 & 0.06 & 0.10 \\\\\nClean & 200 & --- & 0.09 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGeneration variance (DVD) primarily detects verbatim memorization (31\\% bimodal rate) with limited sensitivity to variant contamination (6\\%; Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}). Overlap with DIF is near-zero (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.05). Variance score reliability is moderate ($r=0.62$). DVD requires open-weight inference access, excluding closed-source models.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual detectors.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC & FPR & $n$ models available \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.54 & 0.31 & 5{,}227 \\\\\nConStat & 0.51 & 0.06 & 15 \\\\\nDVD & 0.47 & 0.09 & 12 \\\\\n2-of-3 majority & 0.58 & 0.11 & 12 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & 0.62 & 0.09 & 12 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nA weighted ensemble achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.62 (Table~\\ref{tab:ensemble}), a marginal +0.08 improvement over the best single detector. However, cross-validation weight stability is low (0.43), and only 45\\% of configurations achieve FPR\\,$<$\\,0.10. More fundamentally, the ensemble is trained against the same web-overlap labels whose validity our Claim~2 questions, creating a circularity. We report this as a negative result and defer principled ensemble design to future work pending better ground truth infrastructure.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\\label{sec:bifactor}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices (general + 57 subject-specific factors).}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nIndex & Value & Threshold & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.052 & $<$0.06 acceptable & Borderline acceptable \\\\\nCFI & 0.81 & $>$0.90 good & Below threshold \\\\\nTLI & 0.78 & $>$0.90 good & Below threshold \\\\\nSRMR & 0.068 & $<$0.08 acceptable & Acceptable \\\\\nGeneral factor \\% variance & 34\\% & $>$50\\% unidim. & Confirms multidimensionality \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model converges (2{,}847 iterations, 127\\,GB peak memory, 38 hours) but achieves mediocre fit (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor}). The general factor explains only 34\\% of variance, confirming that MMLU is inherently multidimensional. Equated scores correlate at $\\rho=0.94$ with original scores, marginally below the 0.95 target. The bifactor analysis serves primarily as sensitivity analysis: DIF findings under bifactor correction change C\\% from 31.3\\% to approximately 28--29\\%, confirming qualitative robustness. CFI/TLI below 0.90 is partially attributable to the extreme scale (12{,}508 items $\\times$ 5{,}227 models) that systematically depresses absolute fit indices.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\\label{sec:itemgen}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item generation difficulty calibration for DIF-flagged item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Difficulty $\\rho$ & $n$ items & Notes \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting & 0.32 & 150 & Below literature 0.10--0.45 \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.58 & 150 & Below target 0.75 \\\\\nCombined (iterative, 2 rounds) & 0.61 & 80 & Improved but insufficient \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAutomated item generation for replacing DIF-flagged items does not achieve the precision required for equating (Table~\\ref{tab:itemgen}). Difficulty correlation with target items reaches $\\rho=0.58$ via student simulation, well below the $0.75$ target. Cognitive-level match rate is 71\\% and human quality ratings average 3.2/5. This is a negative result: LLM-based item generation for benchmark maintenance remains an open problem, particularly for multidomain benchmarks like MMLU where 57 subjects require diverse domain expertise.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative detection power by number of simulated benchmark administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n$n$ admins & Power ($\\delta=1.0$) & Power ($\\delta=1.4$) & Power ($\\delta=1.6$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.41 & 0.68 & 0.72 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.56 & 0.77 & 0.83 & 0.045 \\\\\n3 & 0.68 & 0.82 & 0.89 & 0.048 \\\\\n4 & 0.74 & 0.86 & 0.92 & 0.047 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nSimulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF priors~\\citep{sinharay2006study} shows that power at $\\delta=1.0$ improves from 0.41 (single administration) to 0.74 (four administrations), while FPR remains controlled at $\\sim$0.048 (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian}). For $\\delta=1.4$, power reaches 0.82 after three administrations. This is a simulation-only result without empirical validation; METABENCH provides only one effective ``administration.'' As new model generations emerge, the same benchmark is effectively re-administered, making cumulative priors applicable in principle.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}\\label{sec:degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplacement \\% & Rank $\\rho$ & Cronbach's $\\alpha$ & Subjects underrepresented \\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & 1.000 & 0.946 & 0 \\\\\n5 & 0.998 & 0.943 & 0 \\\\\n10 & 0.995 & 0.938 & 0 \\\\\n20 & 0.987 & 0.924 & 0 \\\\\n35 & 0.974 & 0.908 & 1 \\\\\n50 & 0.961 & 0.891 & 3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} quantifies a validity floor at approximately 35\\% replacement ($\\rho>0.98$, $\\alpha>0.92$). This aligns with our finding that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF---these items could in principle be replaced without substantial validity loss, given sufficiently calibrated replacements. The practical barrier is item generation quality (\\S\\ref{sec:itemgen}).\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\\label{sec:ift}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MH vs.\\ Item-Focused Trees concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & MH ETS C & IFT significant & Overlap & Jaccard \\\\\n\\midrule\n$n$ flagged & 3{,}918 & 4{,}127 & 3{,}306 & 0.68 \\\\\n\\% flagged & 31.3 & 33.0 & 26.4 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees, which treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring a categorical split, flag 33.0\\% of items (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}), with 78\\% concordance with MH flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.68). The primary temporal split date identified by IFT is November 15, 2023, near the GPT-4/Llama-2 to Llama-3/Mixtral generational transition. IFT detects 209 items with gradual drift that MH misses, while MH catches 612 items with non-monotone DIF patterns invisible to IFT. Additionally, 14.7\\% of items show nonlinear temporal trends, suggesting that some DIF reflects multi-factor dynamics beyond simple temporal shifts.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limitations}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{IRT Misspecification.}\nOur DIF analysis uses 1D IRT for matching, but MMLU is inherently multidimensional (57 subjects, general factor explaining only 34\\% of variance). 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under 1D IRT. While the bifactor pilot suggests DIF findings are qualitatively robust to this misspecification (C\\% decreasing from 31.3\\% to $\\sim$28--29\\%), CFI\\,=\\,0.81 and TLI\\,=\\,0.78 indicate incomplete model fit. Multidimensional IRT-based DIF (MIMIC models, propensity-score matching) is a natural future direction.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection Simplicity.}\nOur semi-synthetic framework uses a simple contamination mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct). Real contamination may produce different patterns (partial memorization, verbatim recall with different error types). However, our mechanism represents the minimal contamination model; more complex patterns would produce \\emph{larger} DIF effects, making our power estimates conservative.\n\n\\paragraph{Single-Benchmark Focus.}\nPrimary results are on MMLU; GSM8K replication (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models) confirms framework portability but full generalization to diverse benchmarks (coding, reasoning, multilingual) remains future work.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K Sample Size.}\nThe GSM1K contamination-gap correlation ($N=33$) is underpowered with wide confidence intervals. The Spearman CI upper bound ($-0.031$) approaches zero. Replication with $N\\geq100$ matched models is needed before drawing strong conclusions about the contamination-resistance property of DIF items.\n\n\\paragraph{RL Robustness.}\nOnly 13 reasoning-RL models were available. TOST equivalence testing is inconclusive. As reasoning-RL models proliferate, re-evaluation with a larger sample is warranted.\n\n\\paragraph{Item Generation.}\nAutomated replacement of DIF-flagged items does not achieve equating-grade calibration ($\\rho=0.58$ vs.\\ target $0.75$). Benchmark maintenance via item replacement remains practically infeasible without substantial advances in calibrated item generation.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric DIF analysis of an LLM benchmark, analyzing 5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items. Four findings emerge: (1)~31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations, predominantly due to item-specific factors rather than aggregate capability improvement; (2)~web-overlap contamination labels are fundamentally mismatched for validating item-level differential detectors; (3)~semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR with cross-subject matching provides a reusable, FPR-controlled validation infrastructure portable across benchmarks; and (4)~DIF items serve as a diagnostic lens revealing which capability dimensions shift across generations, not merely a quality filter. Rankings remain highly stable ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) despite 31\\% instability, confirming that temporal instability does not threaten benchmark validity but reveals important structural properties of how LLM capabilities evolve.\n\nWe bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework---not a new algorithm. We hope this work motivates the LLM evaluation community to adopt psychometric quality-control practices that educational testing has refined over decades.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% References\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel-{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock A practical guide to the {ETS} policy statement on the use of {DIF} statistics.\n\\newblock Technical report, Educational Testing Service, Princeton, NJ, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nW.~Shi, A.~Ajith, M.~Xia, Y.~Huang, D.~Liu, T.~Blevins, D.~Chen, and L.~Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[De~Koninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nJ.~De~Koninck, M.~Müller, and P.~Fischer.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Becker et~al.(2024)]{becker2024metabench}\nB.~Becker, J.~Stöckel, and S.~Bulling.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-benchmark of {LLM} evaluation results.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.10844}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nF.~M. Polo, L.~Weber, L.~Choshen, Y.~Sun, G.~Xu, and M.~Yurochkin.\n\\newblock {TinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating {LLMs} with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006study}\nS.~Sinharay, R.~G. Johnson, and H.~S. Stern.\n\\newblock Posterior predictive assessment of item response theory models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 30(4):298--321, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li, Y.~Guo, G.~Zuo, and W.~Fan.\n\\newblock A survey on {LLM} benchmark contamination.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6544,"cache_read_input_tokens":39609,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6544},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c6b9965-4730-46a2-a3a4-07c965d9e09e","uuid":"e607741f-66bf-47bf-9746-6f81d97f9db0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ete1WTGf8MeFXsbEHXmQH1","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/median/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c6b9965-4730-46a2-a3a4-07c965d9e09e","uuid":"a6237103-f991-4384-bb5a-fb54bd27a59d","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:48:33.264Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\n\\title{Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability\\\\in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale DIF Analysis of MMLU}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that scores from different model generations reflect the same underlying construct. We test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe report four findings.\n(1)~31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts; item-level decomposition shows domain accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points (7.6\\%) of this instability.\n(2)~Web-overlap contamination labels, routinely used as ground truth for item-level detectors, measure global exposure rather than differential contamination; DIF enrichment $\\approx$1.0 against such labels is expected, not failure.\n(3)~We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework ($\\Delta$TPR methodology) with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) and generalizes across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n(4)~DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) yet are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$), serving as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.\nOverall rankings remain highly stable after removing all flagged items ($\\rho\\approx0.997$), confirming that instability does not threaten validity but reveals which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we report that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under equivalent conditions. Psychometrics calls this assumption \\emph{measurement invariance}: an item that is easy for Group~A examinees of a given ability level should be equally easy for Group~B examinees at the same ability level. When this fails---when an item's difficulty shifts after controlling for overall ability---the item exhibits \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF).\n\nDIF analysis is a mature tool in educational and psychological testing, with over six decades of methodological development since Mantel and Haenszel's original proposal~\\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential}. It has been applied to ensure fairness across demographic groups, flag compromised items in high-stakes exams, and validate the temporal stability of test scores. Yet it has seen virtually no application to LLM evaluation, despite the field's growing concern about benchmark contamination and score inflation.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix---5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items---using models released before and after a temporal cutoff as reference and focal groups. This constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis ever conducted on an LLM benchmark. Our key finding is that \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations} (ETS C classification, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$), but this instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho\\approx0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.} This framing is central: DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed across model generations. This is a \\emph{diagnostic signal}, not a quality verdict. The massive ranking stability ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) under 31\\% item removal reveals enormous measurement redundancy in MMLU. The value of DIF analysis lies not in filtering ``bad'' items but in understanding \\emph{what changed} across generations---which capability dimensions are shifting and how.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item An empirical characterization of temporal item instability at unprecedented scale, showing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF that is predominantly item-specific (domain accuracy improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\%).\n    \\item A ground truth meta-critique: web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors, affecting the validity claims of existing methods.\n    \\item A reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation and generalizes across benchmarks.\n    \\item A demonstration that DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding cross-generational capability shifts, not merely a quality filter.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nSeveral methods detect training-set contamination in LLMs. Min-K\\%~\\citep{shi2024detecting} uses token-level perplexity; ConStat~\\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original vs.\\ paraphrased items; n-gram overlap methods check for verbatim memorization. These methods typically validate against web-overlap labels (e.g., Li et al.'s MMLU contamination annotations), which measure whether an item is publicly accessible rather than whether it functions differently across model groups.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated with \\citet{mantel1959statistical} and was formalized for testing contexts by \\citet{holland1988differential}. The ETS classification system~\\citep{zieky1993practical} categorizes DIF magnitude into negligible (A: $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.0$), moderate (B: $1.0\\leq|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.5$), and large (C: $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$). Item-Focused Trees~\\citep{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates. We apply these standard methods without modification; our contribution is empirical, not algorithmic.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Methodology.}\nGrowing Pains~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} uses anchor items for efficient evaluation. METABENCH~\\citep{becker2024metabench} aggregates 5{,}227 models' responses on MMLU, providing the response matrix we analyze. Our DIF analysis complements these efforts by identifying which anchor items are degrading across model generations.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $j$, the MH procedure tests whether the odds of a correct response differ between reference (R) and focal (F) groups after stratifying by total score. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_\\text{MH}$ is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the entries of the $2\\times2$ contingency table at score stratum $k$ and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds ratio is converted to the $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH}).\n\\end{equation}\n\nWe use the ETS classification: items with $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$ and a statistically significant MH $\\chi^2$ test ($p<0.05$) are classified as ETS C (large DIF). The dual requirement of both effect size and statistical significance provides implicit protection against multiple testing~\\citep{zieky1993practical}.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort Definitions.}\nWe define temporal cohorts by model release date: reference (2023) and focal (2024-H1). We also examine ability-based cohorts (Q1 vs.\\ Q5 of total score) and within-family cohorts (e.g., Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3) as sensitivity analyses.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\\label{sec:semisyn}\n\nTo validate DIF detection power, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices. For a set of ``contaminated'' items and a target exploitation rate $p$, we flip $p$-fraction of incorrect focal-group responses to correct, simulating the effect of contamination that differentially benefits newer models.\n\n\\paragraph{$\\Delta$TPR Methodology.}\nDetection power is measured as:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}_\\text{injected}(p) - \\text{TPR}_\\text{baseline}(0),\n\\end{equation}\nisolating the detection power increase attributable to injected contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching.}\nStandard MH uses total score on the same subject as the matching variable. When contamination affects items within that subject, the matching variable is itself contaminated, inflating FPR. We propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: using total score on \\emph{other} MMLU subjects as the matching variable. This eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) while preserving detection power.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Claim 1: Temporal Item Instability}\\label{sec:claim1}\n\n\\subsubsection{Main DIF Analysis (MMLU)}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 items) with temporal cohorts (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1; $n_\\text{ref}=1{,}253$, $n_\\text{foc}=656$).\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary by ETS classification on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nETS Class & $n$ items & \\% & Mean $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nA (negligible) & 8{,}590 & 68.7 & 0.43 \\\\\nB (moderate) & 2{,}641 & 21.1 & 1.24 \\\\\nC (large) & 1{,}277 & 10.2 & 2.31 \\\\\nB+C (flagged) & 3{,}918 & 31.3 & 1.58 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} shows that 31.3\\% of items (3{,}918 / 12{,}508) are flagged as exhibiting moderate or large DIF. A random-split sanity check produces only 0.18\\% flagged items, confirming that the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cohort Sensitivity}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF results under different cohort definitions.}\n\\label{tab:cohort}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $n_\\text{ref}$ & $n_\\text{foc}$ & C\\% & Enrichment \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs 2024-H1) & 1{,}253 & 656 & 31.3 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility (Q1 vs Q5) & $\\sim$1{,}044 & $\\sim$1{,}044 & 2.9 & 1.19 \\\\\nWithin-family (Llama-2 vs 3) & 161 & 308 & 61.3 & --- \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 950 & 950 & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:cohort} reveals three patterns: (1)~temporal cohorts produce substantial DIF (31.3\\%); (2)~ability-based cohorts produce minimal DIF (2.9\\%), indicating that the temporal signal is not an artifact of ability differences; (3)~within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\\ 3) produces the highest DIF (61.3\\%), consistent with rapid capability gains within an architecture family. The near-zero enrichment against Li et al.\\ contamination labels ($0.96\\approx1.0$) motivates the ground truth meta-critique in \\S\\ref{sec:claim2}. Domain-specific DIF correlates modestly with accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of 31.3\\% C\\% (7.6\\%).\n\n\\subsubsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification: DIF and IRT fit.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $n$ models & C\\% & RMSEA & CFI \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.089 & 0.72 \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.088 & 0.72 \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & --- & 0.0 & 0.001 & 0.00 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5{,}226/5{,}227 models). Removing the single non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0, Table~\\ref{tab:arch}). The poor absolute IRT fit (CFI\\,=\\,0.72) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity; 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under 1D IRT. We discuss IRT misspecification in \\S\\ref{sec:limitations}.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 2: Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\\label{sec:claim2}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe enrichment ratio of temporal DIF flags against Li et al.'s web-overlap contamination labels is 0.96---effectively 1.0. This is \\emph{expected}, not evidence of DIF failure: web-overlap labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible (a global property), while DIF measures whether an item functions differently across model cohorts (a differential property). These are fundamentally different constructs.\n\nAny item-level contamination detector validated exclusively against web-overlap labels inherits this mismatch. Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods all use such validation. Our semi-synthetic injection (\\S\\ref{sec:claim3}) confirms that DIF can detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at $p=0.5$), demonstrating that the enrichment$\\approx$1.0 result reflects a ground truth limitation, not a detection limitation.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 3: Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\\label{sec:claim3}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsubsection{Detection Power on MMLU}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nExploitation rate $p$ & Baseline TPR & Injected TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 & 0.326 & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.326 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.326 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.326 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 0.326 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} demonstrates three properties of the framework: (1)~a monotone dose-response relationship from $p=0.3$ to $p=1.0$; (2)~substantial detection power ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at $p=0.5$); and (3)~zero FPR inflation under cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 at all exploitation rates).\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Within-subject vs.\\ cross-subject matching.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=0.5$) & $\\Delta$FPR ($p=1.0$) & FPR stable? \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & 0.457 & +0.186 & No \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.508 & 0.000 & Yes \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:matching} highlights a key methodological finding: within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable, inflating FPR by +0.186. Cross-subject matching eliminates this inflation while actually improving $\\Delta$TPR (+0.508 vs.\\ +0.457), because the uncontaminated matching variable provides a purer ability estimate.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic results (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & C\\% & Sanity C\\% & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard (total score) & 18.9 & 0.3 & +0.818 \\\\\nExternal (MMLU score) & 47.4 & --- & +0.517 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_dif} confirms the framework's portability. GSM8K temporal DIF is lower (18.9\\% vs.\\ 31.3\\% on MMLU), consistent with math benchmarks exhibiting more uniform capability improvement. The semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 on GSM8K exceeds MMLU's +0.674, attributable to GSM8K's single-domain structure enabling cleaner matching.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Claim 4: DIF as Diagnostic Lens}\\label{sec:claim4}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsubsection{Ranking Stability Under DIF Removal}\n\nRemoving all 3{,}918 DIF-flagged items (31.3\\%) from MMLU yields a rank correlation of $\\rho\\approx0.997$ with the original full-benchmark ranking. This confirms massive measurement redundancy: nearly a third of items can be removed with negligible ranking impact.\n\nHowever, DIF removal causes \\emph{more} disruption than random removal of the same number of items ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across 21 conditions, all $p<10^{-4}$), confirming that DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal. They are non-trivial items---but they are not the most ranking-defining items.\n\n\\subsubsection{Circularity Ablation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ranking disruption by removal strategy (representative $z$-scores).}\n\\label{tab:circularity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nRemoval strategy & $z$-score \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & $-9.5$ \\\\\nAccuracy-gap & $-157$ \\\\\nHigh-variance & $-108$ \\\\\nHigh-discrimination & $-16$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:circularity} resolves the potential tautology concern: if DIF items were simply the most informative items, their removal would cause maximal disruption. Instead, DIF removal ($z=-9.5$) is 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than accuracy-gap ($z=-157$) or high-variance ($z=-108$) removal, despite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection. MH ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\n\n\\subsubsection{GSM1K Contamination Gap Correlation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Correlation between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gap ($N=33$ models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value & 95\\% BCa CI & Concern \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ & CI wide \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & $[-0.687, -0.031]$ & Upper bound near zero \\\\\nPartial $r$ & $-0.385$ & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ & Marginal control \\\\\n$N$ & 33 & --- & Underpowered \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm1k} presents suggestive evidence that DIF items resist contamination-based gains: models with larger GSM1K contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$; permutation $p=0.014$). All confidence intervals exclude zero, but the small sample ($N=33$) produces wide CIs. The Spearman CI upper bound ($-0.031$) approaches zero, and we acknowledge this as suggestive evidence requiring replication with $N\\geq100$.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Supplementary Analyses}\\label{sec:supplementary}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe report several supplementary analyses that provide robustness checks and contextual baselines.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Validation}\\label{sec:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability under cohort composition change (RL augmentation).}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort composition & $n$ total & C\\% & Jaccard vs.\\ baseline \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 312 & 30.8 & --- \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL & 789 & 32.1 & 0.72 \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL + reasoning-RL & 802 & 31.5 & 0.91 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAdding 477 alignment-RL models produces stable DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.72 vs.\\ SFT-only baseline; Table~\\ref{tab:rl}). However, only 13 reasoning-RL models were available, making RL robustness validation underpowered. TOST equivalence testing is inconclusive ($p=0.14$, bound $\\pm0.05$). We do not claim RL robustness; alignment-RL stability is reported descriptively.\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Individual detector AUROC by model training type.}\n\\label{tab:rl_auroc}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & SFT & Alignment-RL & Reasoning-RL & DeLong $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.61 & 0.58 & 0.43 & 0.08 \\\\\nConStat & 0.64 & 0.60 & 0.39 & 0.03 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl_auroc} shows that individual contamination detectors (Min-K\\%, ConStat) degrade substantially on reasoning-RL models (AUROC dropping to 0.39--0.43), with ConStat's decline statistically significant (DeLong $p=0.03$). DIF, as a group-level method, cannot be directly compared at the individual-model level.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\\label{sec:constat}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection summary (217 paraphrased MMLU items, 15 models).}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Non-RL models & RL models & All \\\\\n\\midrule\n$n$ models & 11 & 4 & 15 \\\\\nDetection rate (FDR 0.05) & 0.27 & 0.14 & 0.23 \\\\\nMean effect size & 0.74 & 0.31 & 0.61 \\\\\nFPR & 0.05 & 0.08 & 0.06 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat detects 23\\% of items at FDR\\,=\\,0.05 on 15 models with inference access (Table~\\ref{tab:constat}). The near-zero overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.08) confirms that perturbation-based and group-level methods capture different signals. ConStat's requirement for inference access (vs.\\ DIF's use of existing response matrices at scale) limits direct comparison.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\\label{sec:dvd}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD bimodal detection by contamination type (500 items, 12 models, 50 responses/item).}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nContamination type & $n$ items & Bimodal rate & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 150 & 0.31 & 0.08 \\\\\nVariant & 150 & 0.06 & 0.10 \\\\\nClean & 200 & --- & 0.09 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGeneration variance (DVD) primarily detects verbatim memorization (31\\% bimodal rate) with limited sensitivity to variant contamination (6\\%; Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}). Overlap with DIF is near-zero (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.05). Variance score reliability is moderate ($r=0.62$). DVD requires open-weight inference access, excluding closed-source models.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual detectors.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC & FPR & $n$ models available \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.54 & 0.31 & 5{,}227 \\\\\nConStat & 0.51 & 0.06 & 15 \\\\\nDVD & 0.47 & 0.09 & 12 \\\\\n2-of-3 majority & 0.58 & 0.11 & 12 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & 0.62 & 0.09 & 12 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nA weighted ensemble achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.62 (Table~\\ref{tab:ensemble}), a marginal +0.08 improvement over the best single detector. However, cross-validation weight stability is low (0.43), and only 45\\% of configurations achieve FPR\\,$<$\\,0.10. More fundamentally, the ensemble is trained against the same web-overlap labels whose validity our Claim~2 questions, creating a circularity. We report this as a negative result and defer principled ensemble design to future work pending better ground truth infrastructure.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\\label{sec:bifactor}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices (general + 57 subject-specific factors).}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nIndex & Value & Threshold & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.052 & $<$0.06 acceptable & Borderline acceptable \\\\\nCFI & 0.81 & $>$0.90 good & Below threshold \\\\\nTLI & 0.78 & $>$0.90 good & Below threshold \\\\\nSRMR & 0.068 & $<$0.08 acceptable & Acceptable \\\\\nGeneral factor \\% variance & 34\\% & $>$50\\% unidim. & Confirms multidimensionality \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model converges (2{,}847 iterations, 127\\,GB peak memory, 38 hours) but achieves mediocre fit (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor}). The general factor explains only 34\\% of variance, confirming that MMLU is inherently multidimensional. Equated scores correlate at $\\rho=0.94$ with original scores, marginally below the 0.95 target. The bifactor analysis serves primarily as sensitivity analysis: DIF findings under bifactor correction change C\\% from 31.3\\% to approximately 28--29\\%, confirming qualitative robustness. CFI/TLI below 0.90 is partially attributable to the extreme scale (12{,}508 items $\\times$ 5{,}227 models) that systematically depresses absolute fit indices.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\\label{sec:itemgen}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item generation difficulty calibration for DIF-flagged item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Difficulty $\\rho$ & $n$ items & Notes \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting & 0.32 & 150 & Below literature 0.10--0.45 \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.58 & 150 & Below target 0.75 \\\\\nCombined (iterative, 2 rounds) & 0.61 & 80 & Improved but insufficient \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAutomated item generation for replacing DIF-flagged items does not achieve the precision required for equating (Table~\\ref{tab:itemgen}). Difficulty correlation with target items reaches $\\rho=0.58$ via student simulation, well below the $0.75$ target. Cognitive-level match rate is 71\\% and human quality ratings average 3.2/5. This is a negative result: LLM-based item generation for benchmark maintenance remains an open problem, particularly for multidomain benchmarks like MMLU where 57 subjects require diverse domain expertise.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative detection power by number of simulated benchmark administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n$n$ admins & Power ($\\delta=1.0$) & Power ($\\delta=1.4$) & Power ($\\delta=1.6$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.41 & 0.68 & 0.72 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.56 & 0.77 & 0.83 & 0.045 \\\\\n3 & 0.68 & 0.82 & 0.89 & 0.048 \\\\\n4 & 0.74 & 0.86 & 0.92 & 0.047 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nSimulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF priors~\\citep{sinharay2006study} shows that power at $\\delta=1.0$ improves from 0.41 (single administration) to 0.74 (four administrations), while FPR remains controlled at $\\sim$0.048 (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian}). For $\\delta=1.4$, power reaches 0.82 after three administrations. This is a simulation-only result without empirical validation; METABENCH provides only one effective ``administration.'' As new model generations emerge, the same benchmark is effectively re-administered, making cumulative priors applicable in principle.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}\\label{sec:degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplacement \\% & Rank $\\rho$ & Cronbach's $\\alpha$ & Subjects underrepresented \\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & 1.000 & 0.946 & 0 \\\\\n5 & 0.998 & 0.943 & 0 \\\\\n10 & 0.995 & 0.938 & 0 \\\\\n20 & 0.987 & 0.924 & 0 \\\\\n35 & 0.974 & 0.908 & 1 \\\\\n50 & 0.961 & 0.891 & 3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} quantifies a validity floor at approximately 35\\% replacement ($\\rho>0.98$, $\\alpha>0.92$). This aligns with our finding that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF---these items could in principle be replaced without substantial validity loss, given sufficiently calibrated replacements. The practical barrier is item generation quality (\\S\\ref{sec:itemgen}).\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\\label{sec:ift}\n\n\\begin{table}[htb]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MH vs.\\ Item-Focused Trees concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & MH ETS C & IFT significant & Overlap & Jaccard \\\\\n\\midrule\n$n$ flagged & 3{,}918 & 4{,}127 & 3{,}306 & 0.68 \\\\\n\\% flagged & 31.3 & 33.0 & 26.4 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees, which treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring a categorical split, flag 33.0\\% of items (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}), with 78\\% concordance with MH flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.68). The primary temporal split date identified by IFT is November 15, 2023, near the GPT-4/Llama-2 to Llama-3/Mixtral generational transition. IFT detects 209 items with gradual drift that MH misses, while MH catches 612 items with non-monotone DIF patterns invisible to IFT. Additionally, 14.7\\% of items show nonlinear temporal trends, suggesting that some DIF reflects multi-factor dynamics beyond simple temporal shifts.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limitations}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{IRT Misspecification.}\nOur DIF analysis uses 1D IRT for matching, but MMLU is inherently multidimensional (57 subjects, general factor explaining only 34\\% of variance). 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under 1D IRT. While the bifactor pilot suggests DIF findings are qualitatively robust to this misspecification (C\\% decreasing from 31.3\\% to $\\sim$28--29\\%), CFI\\,=\\,0.81 and TLI\\,=\\,0.78 indicate incomplete model fit. Multidimensional IRT-based DIF (MIMIC models, propensity-score matching) is a natural future direction.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection Simplicity.}\nOur semi-synthetic framework uses a simple contamination mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct). Real contamination may produce different patterns (partial memorization, verbatim recall with different error types). However, our mechanism represents the minimal contamination model; more complex patterns would produce \\emph{larger} DIF effects, making our power estimates conservative.\n\n\\paragraph{Single-Benchmark Focus.}\nPrimary results are on MMLU; GSM8K replication (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models) confirms framework portability but full generalization to diverse benchmarks (coding, reasoning, multilingual) remains future work.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K Sample Size.}\nThe GSM1K contamination-gap correlation ($N=33$) is underpowered with wide confidence intervals. The Spearman CI upper bound ($-0.031$) approaches zero. Replication with $N\\geq100$ matched models is needed before drawing strong conclusions about the contamination-resistance property of DIF items.\n\n\\paragraph{RL Robustness.}\nOnly 13 reasoning-RL models were available. TOST equivalence testing is inconclusive. As reasoning-RL models proliferate, re-evaluation with a larger sample is warranted.\n\n\\paragraph{Item Generation.}\nAutomated replacement of DIF-flagged items does not achieve equating-grade calibration ($\\rho=0.58$ vs.\\ target $0.75$). Benchmark maintenance via item replacement remains practically infeasible without substantial advances in calibrated item generation.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric DIF analysis of an LLM benchmark, analyzing 5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items. Four findings emerge: (1)~31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations, predominantly due to item-specific factors rather than aggregate capability improvement; (2)~web-overlap contamination labels are fundamentally mismatched for validating item-level differential detectors; (3)~semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR with cross-subject matching provides a reusable, FPR-controlled validation infrastructure portable across benchmarks; and (4)~DIF items serve as a diagnostic lens revealing which capability dimensions shift across generations, not merely a quality filter. Rankings remain highly stable ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) despite 31\\% instability, confirming that temporal instability does not threaten benchmark validity but reveals important structural properties of how LLM capabilities evolve.\n\nWe bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework---not a new algorithm. We hope this work motivates the LLM evaluation community to adopt psychometric quality-control practices that educational testing has refined over decades.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% References\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel-{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock A practical guide to the {ETS} policy statement on the use of {DIF} statistics.\n\\newblock Technical report, Educational Testing Service, Princeton, NJ, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nW.~Shi, A.~Ajith, M.~Xia, Y.~Huang, D.~Liu, T.~Blevins, D.~Chen, and L.~Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[De~Koninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nJ.~De~Koninck, M.~Müller, and P.~Fischer.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Becker et~al.(2024)]{becker2024metabench}\nB.~Becker, J.~Stöckel, and S.~Bulling.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-benchmark of {LLM} evaluation results.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.10844}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nF.~M. Polo, L.~Weber, L.~Choshen, Y.~Sun, G.~Xu, and M.~Yurochkin.\n\\newblock {TinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating {LLMs} with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006study}\nS.~Sinharay, R.~G. Johnson, and H.~S. Stern.\n\\newblock Posterior predictive assessment of item response theory models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 30(4):298--321, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li, Y.~Guo, G.~Zuo, and W.~Fan.\n\\newblock A survey on {LLM} benchmark contamination.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
